@startuml keycloak-registration
title Registration - the steps of the handout scenario

autonumber
skinparam shadowing false

participant "AuthController" as Ctl
participant "UserService" as Svc
participant "PersonClient\n(over generated PersonsApi)" as Person
participant "KeycloakClient" as Kcl
participant "person-service" as Ps
participant "Keycloak" as Kc

Ctl -> Svc : register(RegistrationRequest)
activate Svc

== 1. the domain user is created first ==

Svc -> Person : createPerson(email, firstName, lastName)
activate Person
Person -> Ps : POST /api/v1/persons/registration\n{ "email", "firstName", "lastName" }
Ps --> Person : 201 { "userUid": "6f1d2a4e-..." }
Person --> Svc : userUid
deactivate Person

note right of Svc
  The password never travels here.
  person-service owns the profile,
  Keycloak owns the credentials.

  This step comes first on purpose:
  if it fails, no Keycloak account
  was created, so nothing is left
  half-made.
end note

== 2. this service authenticates as itself ==

Svc -> Kcl : createAccount(email, firstName, lastName, userUid)
activate Kcl

Kcl -> Kcl : adminLogin()
note right of Kcl
  Service-account token (client_credentials),
  cached until 10 s before it expires, so most
  calls skip this request entirely.
end note
Kcl -> Kc : POST /realms/{realm}/protocol/openid-connect/token\nform: grant_type=client_credentials\n      client_id, client_secret
Kc --> Kcl : 200 { access_token, expires_in }

== 3. the account, without a password yet ==

Kcl -> Kc : POST /admin/realms/{realm}/users\nAuthorization: Bearer <admin token>
activate Kc
note right of Kc
  {
    "username": "user@dezxxx.com",
    "email":    "user@dezxxx.com",
    "firstName": "Ivan",
    "lastName":  "Ivanov",
    "enabled": true,
    "emailVerified": false,
    "attributes": { "user_uid": ["6f1d2a4e-..."] }
  }

  No credentials here - the handout
  sets the password separately.
  user_uid rides along as a custom
  attribute.
end note
Kc --> Kcl : 201 Created\nLocation: .../users/0b7a5f2c-19d4-...\n(no body at all)
deactivate Kc

note right of Kcl
  The new id arrives only in the
  Location header. Parsing it out
  is this client's job.
end note

Kcl --> Svc : keycloakUserId
deactivate Kcl

== 4. the password ==

Svc -> Kcl : resetUserPassword(keycloakUserId, password)
activate Kcl
Kcl -> Kc : PUT /admin/realms/{realm}/users/{id}/reset-password\n{ "type": "password",\n  "value": "Str0ngP@ssw0rd",\n  "temporary": false }
Kc --> Kcl : 204 No Content
Kcl --> Svc : done
deactivate Kcl

note over Kcl, Kc
  temporary MUST be false. Left at Keycloak's
  default the account is created successfully
  and the very next login fails with
  400 (Bad Request) / invalid_grant
  "Account is not fully set up".
end note

== 5. the platform role ==

Svc -> Kcl : assignPlatformRole(keycloakUserId)
activate Kcl
Kcl -> Kc : GET /admin/realms/{realm}/roles/USER
Kc --> Kcl : 200 { id, name }
Kcl -> Kc : POST /admin/realms/{realm}/users/{id}/role-mappings/realm\n[ { id, name } ]
Kc --> Kcl : 204 No Content
Kcl --> Svc : done
deactivate Kcl

note over Kcl, Kc
  The realm import hands out no role: neither a
  realm default role nor realmRoles in the create
  body works - Keycloak accepts and ignores them.
  Two calls, because a mapping is resolved by id
  and the id is known only after reading the role.
end note

== 6. an ordinary login, nothing special ==

Svc -> Kcl : login(email, password)
activate Kcl
Kcl -> Kc : POST /realms/{realm}/protocol/openid-connect/token\nform: grant_type=password
Kc --> Kcl : 200 { access_token, refresh_token, expires_in, token_type }
Kcl --> Svc : TokenResponse
deactivate Kcl

== 7. the answer ==

Svc -> Svc : response.userUid(userUid)
Svc --> Ctl : 201 { accessToken, refreshToken,\n     expiresIn, tokenType, userUid }
deactivate Svc

@enduml


@startuml keycloak-rollback
title What happens when the password or the role step fails

skinparam shadowing false

start

:person-service created the domain user;
:Keycloak created the account
(no password yet);

if (password set and role granted?) then (yes)
  :login and answer the client;
  stop
else (no)
  note right
    The account exists but can never
    be logged into, and the address is
    already taken: every later attempt
    to register it answers
    409 (Conflict) - forever.
  end note
  :KeycloakClient.rollbackAccountWithError
  DELETE /admin/realms/{realm}/users/{id};
  note right
    Compensating action. The address is
    free again, the caller can simply
    try once more.

    The domain user in person-service is
    NOT rolled back - its contract has no
    delete operation. Recorded as a known
    gap in CONTEXT.md.
  end note
  :answer the client with the original failure
  (as REGISTRATION_INCONSISTENT);
  stop
endif

@enduml


@startuml keycloak-me
title GET /api/v1/auth/me - why the Admin API is needed here too

skinparam shadowing false

rectangle "Access token (already verified)" as Tok {
  card "sub            -> keycloakUserId" as c1
  card "email          -> email" as c2
  card "given_name     -> firstName" as c3
  card "family_name    -> lastName" as c4
  card "email_verified -> emailVerified" as c5
  card "realm_access.roles -> roles" as c6
  card "user_uid       -> userUid" as c7
}

rectangle "Admin API\nGET /admin/realms/{realm}/users/{sub}" as Adm {
  card "createdTimestamp -> registeredAt" as c8
}

rectangle "CurrentUserResponse\n8 required fields" as Out

Tok --> Out : seven fields, no network call
Adm --> Out : the eighth one\n(KeycloakClient.findRegisteredAt)

note bottom of Tok
  user_uid is in the token because the realm
  declares a protocol mapper for it. Without
  that mapper this field would need a second
  admin call.
end note

note bottom of Adm
  registeredAt exists nowhere in the token,
  so /me costs one admin read. The alternative
  would be storing it on our side, and this
  service owns no database.
end note

@enduml


@startuml keycloak-classes
title Where KeycloakClient sits

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0

package service {
  class UserService
  class TokenService
}

package "gateway.person" {
  class PersonClient <<@Component>> {
    + Mono<UUID> createPerson(email,\n                       firstName, lastName)
    - translate(error)
  }
}

package "gateway.keycloak.client" {
  class KeycloakClient <<@Component>> {
    .. token endpoint ..
    + Mono<TokenResponse> login(email, password)
    + Mono<TokenResponse> refresh(refreshToken)
    + Mono<String> adminLogin()
    .. Admin API ..
    + Mono<String> createAccount(email, firstName,\n                         lastName, userUid)
    + Mono<Void> resetUserPassword(id, password)
    + Mono<Void> assignPlatformRole(id)
    + Mono<T> rollbackAccountWithError(id, cause)
    + Mono<OffsetDateTime> findRegisteredAt(id)
    .. errors ..
    - translate(response)
  }
  class KeycloakUserRequest <<record>> {
    username, email, firstName, lastName,
    enabled, emailVerified, attributes
  }
  class KeycloakTokenResponse <<record>> {
    accessToken, refreshToken,
    expiresIn, tokenType
  }
}

package util {
  class GatewayErrors <<static>> {
    + transportFailures(dependency, baseUrl)
  }
}

package config {
  class KeycloakProperties <<record>> {
    tokenUri()
    usersUri()
    rolesUri()
  }
  class HttpClientsConfig {
    keycloakWebClient
    personsApi
  }
}

UserService --> PersonClient : step 1, user_uid
UserService --> KeycloakClient : account, password,\nrole, rollback, login, /me
TokenService --> KeycloakClient : login, refresh

KeycloakClient ..> KeycloakUserRequest : body of POST /users
KeycloakClient ..> KeycloakTokenResponse : token endpoint answer
KeycloakClient ..> GatewayErrors
PersonClient ..> GatewayErrors
KeycloakClient --> KeycloakProperties : tokenUri(), usersUri()
HttpClientsConfig --> KeycloakClient : keycloakWebClient

note right of KeycloakClient
  One class for everything Keycloak, as in the
  teacher's component diagram. The small bodies
  live inside it as private records:
  KeycloakCredential, KeycloakRealmRole,
  KeycloakUserRepresentation, KeycloakError,
  CachedToken.

  translate() turns Keycloak's error answers into
  our ApiException: invalid_grant -> 401, a wrong
  client secret -> 500, 409 -> USER_ALREADY_EXISTS,
  5xx -> 503. The same table for both APIs.
end note

@enduml
